- a new application with 2 components and routing\r\n- components’ HTML is just a link back and forth\r\n- a property eating a lot of space in component B so it’s easy to see the leak\r\n- Chrome’s performance monitoring\r\n\r\nWhen you navigate away from component B, and run GC manually the heap size does not shrink back (since I used _big_ properties it's a 50MB difference).\r\n\r\nFor all observations and scenarios I tried see [this dramatic-titled article](https://medium.com/@zsolt.deak/angular-is-leaking-776d149406210)\r\n\r\nLive: https://blitz-1a72a.firebaseapp.com/ \\\r\nStackBlitz: https://stackblitz.com/edit/stackblitz-starters-ljmfcl?file=src%2Fsecond.component.ts\r\n\r\n### Please provide a link to a minimal reproduction of the bug\r\n\r\nhttps://blitz-1a72a.firebaseapp.com/\r\n\r\n### Please provide the exception or error you saw\r\n\r\n_No response_\r\n\r\n### Please provide the environment you discovered this bug in (run `ng version`)\r\n\r\n```true\r\nAngular 17.1.0\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n### Anything else?\r\n\r\nSince the supposed leak is not growing every time I navigate back and forth I'm suspecting some sort of caching. But child component's of B are properly cleaned up, B's DOM objects (nodes) are also properly cleaned up (unless there is a ViewChild in B, see the `@for` I added and the slight memory decrease when navigating back), so I'm wondering if it really is some sort of caching how much sense does it make to partially cache destroyed routed components? How many cache hits would there be, are top level routing components always initialised with the same data? (with random string I made sure there is cache hit in my case)","closed_by":{"login":"jessicajaniuk","id":72768744,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjcyNzY4NzQ0","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/72768744?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jessicajaniuk","html_url":"https://github.com/jessicajaniuk","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jessicajaniuk/f